WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.535
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-nominated actress

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.670
and writer you know from
her work in "The Big Sick,"

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:07.173
"The Plot Against America,"
and "Olive Kitteridge."

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:10.209
She stars in "She Said," which
is in theaters November 18th.

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:11.678
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:14.380
-What is it exactly
that we're looking at here?

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.351
-We're looking at extreme sexual
harassment in the workplace.

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:19.786
These young women walked into

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:22.588
what they all had reason to
believe were business meetings

00:00:22.588 --> 00:00:24.357
with a producer, an employer.

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:25.525
They were hopeful.

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:27.426
They were expecting
a serious conversation

00:00:27.426 --> 00:00:29.629
about their work
or a possible project.

00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:33.500
Instead, they say he met them
with threats and sexual demands.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.202
They claim assault and rape.

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:38.505
If that can happen
to Hollywood actresses,

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:40.439
who else is it happening to?

00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:43.576
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Zoe Kazan, everybody.

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.545
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:51.984 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:51.984 --> 00:00:58.291 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:58.291 --> 00:00:59.459
Welcome back!

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:01.160
-Thank you.
-Congratulations on the movie.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.195
But more importantly,
congratulations.

00:01:03.195 --> 00:01:07.366
You and your partner, Paul Dano,
had another baby four weeks ago.

00:01:07.366 --> 00:01:08.667
-We did.

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:09.902
-You look amazing.
-Thank you.

00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:10.903
-You seem amazing.

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:12.438
I don't know
if this is all a facade,

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:14.641
but you overall look
like you're doing okay.

00:01:14.641 --> 00:01:16.275
-Thank you.
About five minutes ago,

00:01:16.275 --> 00:01:18.477
I was in your dressing room
with, like, breast pumps in.

00:01:18.477 --> 00:01:20.480
So, uh, I feel great.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.681
-Okay, good.

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:24.484
I know you have
a 4-year-old, as well.

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:27.152
Accepting to the new sibling
so far?

00:01:27.152 --> 00:01:29.355
-She's very, very sweet to him.

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:33.826
And she's, like, really
expressing her emotions to me.

00:01:33.826 --> 00:01:35.128
-Oh, interesting.

00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:37.329
So anything -- any problem
she has with it,

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:39.098
she's bringing to you,
as opposed to taking it out --

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:41.934
-Yeah, which is fair, 'cause
he's 4 weeks old and I'm 39.

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:43.469
-Yeah, yeah, you're probably --

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:46.605
If she's judging who's the best
one to bring the complaints to,

00:01:46.605 --> 00:01:48.875
very sharp kid.

00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:51.610
So this movie
obviously is based on

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:56.415
an incredible piece of reporting
both that Megan and Jodi did

00:01:56.415 --> 00:01:58.484
and then their book "She Said."

00:01:58.484 --> 00:01:59.918
You had obviously read the book.

00:01:59.918 --> 00:02:02.422
What was it like the first time
you got together --

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:05.224
you and Carey got together
with the people you're playing?

00:02:05.224 --> 00:02:08.894
-Yeah, so Carey and I have been
friends for 14 years.

00:02:08.894 --> 00:02:11.397
So, like, we were going
into this together.

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:13.166
And we were incredibly nervous.

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:16.202
We had, like, a four-way Zoom
because this was, like,

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:18.138
I don't know,
the spring of 2021.

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:20.372
And we, like,
all popped on together

00:02:20.372 --> 00:02:21.874
and we're like, "Surprise!

00:02:21.874 --> 00:02:23.676
Like, this is your life.

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:25.711
We're going to be you."

00:02:25.711 --> 00:02:27.546
Um, I was really intimidated.

00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:29.982
They're, like, Pulitzer
Prize-winning journalists.

00:02:29.982 --> 00:02:33.119
But they were so nice
and so generous with us

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:35.388
and, like, let me ask
all the nosy questions

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:37.022
that I secretly
want to ask everyone.

00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:38.424
-That's great
that you got to do that.

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:40.459
It also was surreal, I heard,

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:42.461
because you were shooting
in Brooklyn.

00:02:42.461 --> 00:02:44.063
They live in Brooklyn.

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:46.733
So you actually ran --
like, they ran into you

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.400
while you were on set
playing them.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.637
-Yeah, like,
Jodi literally ran into us.

00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:52.772
She was out for a run
in Prospect Park,

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:54.908
and we were, like,
shooting in Prospect Park.

00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:57.843
And she texted me, being like,
"This is gonna sound weird,

00:02:57.843 --> 00:03:00.746
but are you shooting
in my neighborhood?"

00:03:00.746 --> 00:03:04.183
And I had to be like,
"Yes, come on in!"

00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:08.488
So we have, like, you know,
sweaty selfies on set.

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:13.092
-What did you learn about
by doing this

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:15.561
the way stories like this
are reported

00:03:15.561 --> 00:03:17.396
that you maybe didn't know?

00:03:17.396 --> 00:03:20.133
-Yeah, I mean, okay, so I had a
kind of, like, I think,

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:23.035
naive vision that, like,
an investigative reporter

00:03:23.035 --> 00:03:25.638
would, like, seek out the truth,
learn the truth,

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:28.374
and then, like, publish it
in a newspaper.

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:30.543
Turns out it's a lot more
complicated than that.

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:34.280
Like, they just needed, like,
verification for everything,

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.416
like, three sources
for everything.

00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:38.517
I think, like, I had to,
like, dismantle my, like,

00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:42.455
LEGO version of being
a "New York Times" reporter.

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:44.389
Yeah.
-One of the things that was --

00:03:44.389 --> 00:03:46.859
I mean, the worst
possible reason

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:48.794
for "The New York Times"
building to be open

00:03:48.794 --> 00:03:50.729
for you to shoot there
is there was a pandemic

00:03:50.729 --> 00:03:52.165
and none of the reporters
were there.

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:53.933
But you did get to shoot in
the "New York Times" building.

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:55.401
-Yeah, we're the first
narrative film

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:57.669
to shoot in
the "New York Times" building,

00:03:57.669 --> 00:03:59.271
and the reason is because

00:03:59.271 --> 00:04:02.207
no one was allowed
to be inside of there.

00:04:02.207 --> 00:04:04.109
Jodi and Megan hadn't been there
for like 18 months

00:04:04.109 --> 00:04:05.378
when we were shooting in there.

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:07.213
So it was a little bit like
being in a building

00:04:07.213 --> 00:04:08.581
where everyone
had been raptured.

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:10.850
Like, there was, like, you know,

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:13.119
Valentine's Day candy
on people's desks

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:15.621
and, like, their heels
under the desk

00:04:15.621 --> 00:04:17.990
and, like, you know,
memos that were dated,

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.126
like, March 9, 2020.

00:04:20.126 --> 00:04:21.728
-Yeah, that say, like,
"Call me. It's important."

00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:23.095
-Yeah, ASAP.

00:04:23.095 --> 00:04:26.966
"Something's happening
in China." Yeah.

00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:29.502
-You mentioned you met Carey
14 years ago.

00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:31.103
Was that professionally?
Did you guys work?

00:04:31.103 --> 00:04:32.271
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:34.507
-Yeah, we were in a play
on Broadway together.

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:36.742
And we shared a dressing room
about the size of your desk.

00:04:36.742 --> 00:04:37.943
-So, I'm so glad you --

00:04:37.943 --> 00:04:39.645
I feel like this is
the great unknown thing

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:40.846
when people go
to Broadway shows.

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:43.082
'Cause they're so grand
and palatial rooms.

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:46.686
But I'm always blown away by
how tiny the dressing rooms are.

00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:50.088
-Yeah, I mean, literally
we had, like, our desk --

00:04:50.088 --> 00:04:52.424
Our, like, desk where we put on
our makeup was this big.

00:04:52.424 --> 00:04:54.827
And we would put our, like,
sleeping bags underneath it

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:57.296
and snuggle up together and both
take a nap between shows

00:04:57.296 --> 00:04:59.298
under truly about this size.

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:01.467
So, you know, like,
if we didn't become friends,

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:03.569
we definitely were going to
become enemies

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:05.704
in that situation.

00:05:05.704 --> 00:05:07.640
-Well, it's certainly --
it's certainly nice

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:08.774
that it worked out this way.
-Yeah.

00:05:08.774 --> 00:05:10.943
-And it must be also nice,

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:12.345
obviously,
telling a story like this

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:14.813
that has been, you know,
both told in the press

00:05:14.813 --> 00:05:15.948
and told via book form --

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:18.750
it's great to do it
as a film, as well.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.319
And are Megan and Jodi --

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:23.489
are they happy with how
you've gone about doing it?

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:26.659
-I mean, if they aren't happy,
they're really acting happy.

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:28.861
They're doing a great job
convincing us.

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.295
No, no, no,
they're being so kind.

00:05:30.295 --> 00:05:33.699
And we've all been
so concerned about --

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:36.068
You know, this is a very
delicate subject matter.

00:05:36.068 --> 00:05:38.304
And I think that there's stuff
about the film

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:40.406
that's really entertaining,
but at the end of the day,

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:43.609
it's also, you know,
very, very heavy

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:44.810
and sensitive subject matter.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:47.379
So my eye has been
so much on, like,

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:49.415
are all these survivors happy

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:52.618
or do they feel okay
about their representation,

00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:54.419
that I kind of forgot
about Jodi and Megan.

00:05:54.419 --> 00:05:58.891
Then I saw them at the premiere
and was like, "Hey!

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:00.459
Hope you think
I did a good job!"

00:06:00.459 --> 00:06:01.461
-I'm sure they did.

00:06:01.461 --> 00:06:02.995
It is always so nice to see you.

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:04.463
Thank you so much
for being with us, Zoe.

00:06:04.463 --> 00:06:05.630
-Thanks for having me.
Thank you.

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:07.100
-Zoe Kazan, everybody!

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.868
"She Says" is in theaters
November 18th.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:11.204
We'll be right back with music
from Paolo Nutini.

